WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.812
A secure RNG roadmap begins by identifying which values are security-sensitive.

2
00:00:04.812 --> 00:00:13.635
Tokens, keys, nonces, IVs, salts, challenges, and identifiers can have very different requirements, so name the use case before choosing the source.

3
00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:20.052
If the value must be unpredictable, use an operating-system CSPRNG or an approved cryptographic library.

4
00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:26.468
Do not substitute deterministic engines, fixed seeds, timestamps, counters, or hand-rolled entropy for security randomness.

5
00:00:26.468 --> 00:00:30.478
Centralize RNG access behind a small reviewed interface where practical.

6
00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:37.296
That interface should define the approved source, error handling, formatting helpers, bounded value behavior, and production restrictions.

7
00:00:37.296 --> 00:00:45.717
Keep deterministic sources available only for controlled tests, and design the consuming code so it can be tested without weakening production

8
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:46.118
behavior.

9
00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:53.738
When output is mapped into a range or encoded as text, preserve the required entropy and avoid biased transformations.

10
00:00:53.738 --> 00:00:57.347
Finally, make secure randomness part of normal engineering governance.

11
00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:59.352
Avoid logging sensitive generated values.

12
00:00:59.352 --> 00:01:03.363
Review call sites, dependencies, platform behavior, configuration, and test hooks.

13
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:06.571
Document what is approved and what is prohibited.

14
00:01:06.571 --> 00:01:12.987
Revisit the design when a product adds a new token, protocol, platform, dependency, or deployment mode.

15
00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:23.414
A useful team habit is to treat every new random value as a design decision until its purpose, source, format, lifetime, and review owner are clear.

16
00:01:23.414 --> 00:01:33.039
Secure random generation is not a last-minute utility call; it is a design responsibility that protects boundaries users rarely see but deeply depend

17
00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:33.440
on.
